   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;margin-bottom:16px;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Segoe UI&#39;,Arial,freesans,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22px\">Hi, Is there any option to use two variables in parameter <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">size</code> in <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">ggplot</code> function? E.g.</p><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;margin-bottom:16px;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Segoe UI&#39;,Arial,freesans,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22px\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">ggplot(mtcars, aes(qsec,drat, size = cyl))+</code><br style=\"box-sizing:border-box\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">geom_point()</code></p><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;margin-bottom:16px;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Segoe UI&#39;,Arial,freesans,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22px\">Obviously it works and <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">size</code> of points depends on <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">cyl</code> variable. But is it any option to add another <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">size</code>variable which can use <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">alpha</code> option e.g.<code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">variable = mpg</code>. I found sth like that:</p><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;margin-bottom:16px;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Segoe UI&#39;,Arial,freesans,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22px\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">ggplot() +</code><br style=\"box-sizing:border-box\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">geom_point(data = mtcars, aes(qsec,drat, size = cyl)) +</code><br style=\"box-sizing:border-box\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">geom_point(data = mtcars, aes(qsec,drat, size = mpg), alpha = .1, colour = &quot;red&quot;)</code></p><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Segoe UI&#39;,Arial,freesans,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:22px\">But when I want to set the <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">size</code> of each variable separately, it is not possible. When there is one size variable normally I use <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">scale_size_contiunous</code> but with two size variables it doesn&#39;t work. I know that using <code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:12px;padding-top:0.2em;padding-bottom:0.2em;background-color:rgba(0,0,0,0.0392157);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px\">scale_size_continuous</code> two times doesn&#39;t change anything. It is possible to achieve appropriate graph?</p></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "9hjIaebZFAAJ",
   "from": "Brandon Hurr",
   "author": "27405c4b711a1080",
   "date": "2015-08-27T17:29:12Z",
   "subject": "Re: geom_point in terms of two 'size' variables",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "My understanding is that only a single scale of each type can be used\r<br>in a plot. Much like Highlander, there can only be one.\r<br>\r<br>In your example, it is setting the scale to cover both cyl and mpg.\r<br>Your dots are reflecting the data quite well if you ask me. I assume\r<br>you want to have the cyl scale and the mpg scale on their own scale so\r<br>they show up better. AFAIK, that can&#39;t be done. BUT... you could try\r<br>rescaling cyl to the mpg scale or vice versa, but then your legend is\r<br>kind of junk.\r<br>\r<br>HTH,\r<br>B\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Thu, Aug 27, 2015 at 9:33 AM, Mikołaj Bogucki\r<br>&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">bogucki...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi, Is there any option to use two variables in parameter size in ggplot\r<br>&gt; function? E.g.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; ggplot(mtcars, aes(qsec,drat, size = cyl))+\r<br>&gt; geom_point()\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Obviously it works and size of points depends on cyl variable. But is it any\r<br>&gt; option to add another sizevariable which can use alpha option e.g.variable =\r<br>&gt; mpg. I found sth like that:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; ggplot() +\r<br>&gt; geom_point(data = mtcars, aes(qsec,drat, size = cyl)) +\r<br>&gt; geom_point(data = mtcars, aes(qsec,drat, size = mpg), alpha = .1, colour =\r<br>&gt; &quot;red&quot;)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; But when I want to set the size of each variable separately, it is not\r<br>&gt; possible. When there is one size variable normally I use\r<br>&gt; scale_size_contiunous but with two size variables it doesn&#39;t work. I know\r<br>&gt; that using scale_size_continuous two times doesn&#39;t change anything. It is\r<br>&gt; possible to achieve appropriate graph?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
